  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1025 PM CST THU NOV 5 2026\n\nThe cold front remains over our far SE counties this evening and\nwill continue to push southeastward overnight. Dense fog has\ndeveloped right along and ahead of the front, and a Dense Fog\nAdvisory has been issued for Pike and Barbour Counties. Some\nimprovement is expected behind the front, but with light winds,\nlow dew point depressions, and recent rainfall, at least patchy \nground fog is expected to develop across the forecast area \novernight into Friday morning. A shortwave trough will also move \nthrough the area but only produce some high clouds, while an upper\nlow moves southeastward over the eastern Great Lakes. A \nsecondary, dry cold front associated with this feature will move \nthrough the area on Friday. This will result in cooler lows Friday\nnight, but winds should stay up enough to prevent any frost \nconcerns. A brief shot of slightly below normal high temperatures \nis then expected for Saturday. Surface high pressure will result \nin favorable radiational cooling conditions Saturday and Sunday \nnights. Chances for frost are medium to high across the far \nnorthern counties and Black Belt, with a low chance for a light \nfreeze in the typically colder locations. Frost advisories will \nlikely be needed when this gets closer.\n\nNo significant weather systems are forecast through early next\nweek. The forecast area will generally be between the subtropical\nand polar jet streams. A southern stream shortwave does move\nacross the area during the Tuesday/Wednesday timeframe. This may\nsqueeze out some virga/sprinkles/very light rain, but low-level\nmoisture return will be lacking and probabilities of measurable\nrain will remain at 10 percent or less. A strengthening ridge\nacross the western and central CONUS will lead to amplification\nof a longwave trough across the eastern CONUS later in the week.\n\n32/JDavis",
